Pretrained multilingual language models, specifically Multilingual BERT (mBERT), enable tasks such as cross-lingual low-resource language modeling and machine translation. However, the extent to which pretraining's benefits transfer between languages remains unclear. Our study investigates mBERT's ability to perform cross-lingual time series forecasting in low-resource settings. We demonstrate that real-valued mBERT encodings are capable of forecasting in languages where no data was used during pretraining, outperforming several benchmarks. We further analyze mBERT's ability to align and forecast data by comparing its English and Spanish encodings and identifying that the first layer of mBERT is more effective in aligning data than real-valued representations. Subsequently, we apply techniques such as fine-tuning and self-training to improve the forecasting performance of real-valued mBERT encodings. Our experiments show that these methods enhance mBERT's forecasting performance, yielding state-of-the-art results in zero-shot and multilingual settings. Our findings indicate that fine-tuning and self-training are effective in forecasting time series across languages used during pretraining, whereas multilingual BERT's cross-lingual language modeling capabilities transfer to forecasting tasks. We hypothesize that the real-valued mBERT encodings develop representations that are more suitable for forecasting time series data. Our study provides new insights into the transferability of pretraining's benefits across languages and highlights the importance of fine-tuning and self-training in improving forecasting performance. The code and data are available at https://github.com/krzysztofszolnai/mBERT-Forecasting. Our study's results are presented in a video at https://youtu.be/6s2kqYqz2sA. The video is available at https://youtu.be/6s2kqYqz2sA. \end{document} 

